---
/**
 * Deliberately NOT generated from src/config/competitors.ts.
 *
 * That template is built for products competing in the same category, and its shape —
 * "where they are better" then "where we differ" — assumes a like-for-like comparison.
 * OpenEvidence is clinical decision support with a scribe feature attached; Stream is a
 * documentation platform with no CDS at all. Running that structure here would imply a
 * competition that does not exist and that we would lose on feature parity.
 *
 * The page this replaces was pulled for exactly that reason. It ranked — 2,451
 * impressions at position 8 — and was still the wrong page, so it was 301'd to the
 * homepage, where it converted almost none of that.
 *
 * Search Console splits the demand almost exactly in half: 281 impressions specifically
 * about OpenEvidence's *scribe*, and 283 for generic "alternative" queries where a
 * documentation tool is not the answer. This page addresses the first half honestly and
 * lets the second half go.
 *
 * DO NOT add claims about clinical decision support to this page. Stream has none
 * active. "What did I miss" is not shipped, and a page hinting otherwise would repeat
 * the failure that PR #27 cleaned up.
 */
import Page from '../../../layouts/Page.astro';

const VERIFIED = '2 September 2026';
const onboardUrl = 'https://stream.riverrecords.ai/onboard/stream-pro?utm_source=comparison&utm_medium=landing&utm_campaign=vs-openevidence-scribe';
---
<Page
  title="OpenEvidence's Scribe and Stream — Different Tools, Often Used Together"
  description="OpenEvidence is clinical decision support with a scribe feature. Stream is a documentation platform. What each is actually for, where they overlap, and why many clinicians use both.">

<Fragment slot="head">
  <script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "headline": "OpenEvidence's scribe and Stream",
    "description": "What each tool is for, where they overlap, and why many clinicians use both.",
    "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "River Records" },
  })} />
</Fragment>

<style>
.oe { padding: 60px 0 88px; }
.oe-wrap { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; }
.oe h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 2.7rem); color: var(--ink); line-height: 1.18; margin-bottom: 16px; }
.oe-lede { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--ink-muted); line-height: 1.7; margin-bottom: 12px; }
.oe-stamp { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.7rem; letter-spacing: 0.06em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-faint); margin-bottom: 36px; padding-bottom: 22px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); }
.oe h2 { font-size: 1.45rem; color: var(--ink); margin: 42px 0 14px; line-height: 1.3; }
.oe p { font-size: 1rem; color: var(--ink-muted); line-height: 1.75; margin-bottom: 16px; }
.oe ul { margin: 0 0 18px; padding-left: 22px; }
.oe li { font-size: 1rem; color: var(--ink-muted); line-height: 1.75; margin-bottom: 9px; }
.oe strong { color: var(--ink); }

.oe-split { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 18px; margin: 26px 0; }
.oe-card { border: 1px solid var(--border); border-radius: var(--r-lg); padding: 24px; background: var(--white); }
.oe-card--them { background: var(--surface); }
.oe-card h3 { font-size: 1.05rem; margin-bottom: 6px; color: var(--ink); font-family: var(--font-sans); }
.oe-card .oe-kind { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.64rem; letter-spacing: 0.1em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--ink-faint); margin-bottom: 10px; display: block; }
.oe-card p { font-size: 0.93rem; margin-bottom: 0; }

.oe-both { background: var(--primary-light); border: 1px solid var(--primary-border); border-radius: var(--r-lg); padding: 26px 28px; margin: 30px 0; }
.oe-both h2 { margin-top: 0; font-size: 1.3rem; }
.oe-both p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

.oe-limit { border-left: 3px solid var(--border-strong); padding: 6px 0 6px 22px; margin: 30px 0; }
.oe-limit p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

.oe-note { font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.7; border-top: 1px solid var(--border); margin-top: 40px; padding-top: 20px; }
.oe-note a { color: var(--primary); }

.oe-cta { background: var(--dark); color: var(--white); border-radius: var(--r-xl); padding: 32px; margin-top: 38px; text-align: center; }
.oe-cta h2 { color: var(--white); margin: 0 0 10px; font-size: 1.45rem; }
.oe-cta p { color: rgba(250,247,240,0.78); margin-bottom: 22px; }
.oe-cta .btn { margin: 0 6px 8px; }

@media (max-width: 720px) { .oe-split { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
</style>

<section class="oe">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="oe-wrap">

      <span class="section-label">Comparison</span>
      <h1>OpenEvidence has a scribe. Stream is a documentation platform. Those are different things.</h1>
      <p class="oe-lede">People searching for “OpenEvidence scribe” are usually trying to work out what OpenEvidence actually does, and whether its scribe is the answer to their documentation problem. This is an honest attempt at that, including where we are not the answer.</p>
      <p class="oe-stamp">Written {VERIFIED} · we do not speak for OpenEvidence — check their site</p>

      <h2>What each one is</h2>
      <div class="oe-split">
        <div class="oe-card oe-card--them">
          <span class="oe-kind">OpenEvidence</span>
          <h3>Clinical decision support</h3>
          <p>A clinical reference tool. You ask a clinical question and it answers from the medical literature with citations. That is the product, and by all accounts it is very good at it. A scribe has been added as a feature alongside it.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="oe-card">
          <span class="oe-kind">Stream</span>
          <h3>A documentation platform</h3>
          <p>The record itself. Visits become a problem-based chart, inbound faxes and outside records are read and filed under the problems they concern, and what accumulates is a chart rather than a stack of notes.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>They overlap in exactly one place: both can turn a conversation into a note. Everything either product does beyond that has almost no relationship to the other.</p>

      <h2>So which one solves your problem?</h2>
      <p>It depends what the problem actually is, and those are two different problems that often get discussed as one.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>“I need to look something up mid-visit.”</strong> That is a clinical-knowledge problem. OpenEvidence is built for it. Stream does nothing here.</li>
        <li><strong>“I am typing notes at 9pm.”</strong> That is a documentation problem. Any competent scribe helps, including theirs.</li>
        <li><strong>“I spend the first five minutes of every visit rebuilding what happened last time.”</strong> That is a chart problem, and it is the one Stream is built for. A scribe of any kind will not fix it, because a note generator has nowhere to put the accumulated context.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="oe-both">
        <h2>Many clinicians should use both</h2>
        <p>This is not a diplomatic hedge. The tools do genuinely different jobs, and a physician who looks things up during visits and also wants a chart that accumulates is well served by having both open.</p>
        <p>If OpenEvidence's scribe already handles your notes and you are content with where they land, you do not need us. That is a real answer, and we would rather say it here than take a trial from someone who will cancel in a month.</p>
      </div>

      <h2>Where Stream is genuinely different</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The chart, not the note.</strong> Each problem carries its own thread — every assessment and plan you have written for it, in order. One glance shows what has been quietly falling off your attention.</li>
        <li><strong>Inbound documents become part of the record.</strong> Faxes, PDFs and pasted records are read, and their clinical content is filed under the problems it belongs to, with every fact linking back to its source page.</li>
        <li><strong>It is organized by medical problem, not by date.</strong> Which is how clinicians think about patients, and not how encounters are usually stored.</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="oe-limit">
        <p><strong>What Stream does not do, plainly.</strong> There is no clinical decision support in Stream. No literature search, no differential suggestions, no answers to clinical questions. If that is what you are looking for, this is not the product and no amount of comparison will change that.</p>
        <p>We also do not write back into your EHR — the note copies over in one click — and we are not SOC 2 certified. If either is a requirement, that is a reason to look elsewhere.</p>
      </div>

      <p class="oe-note">
        OpenEvidence is an independent product and we have no affiliation with them. This page describes our understanding of their offering as of {VERIFIED}; products change, and theirs is developing quickly, so
        <a href="https://www.openevidence.com" rel="nofollow noopener" target="_blank">check their site</a> rather than take our word for what it does today.
      </p>

      <div class="oe-cta">
        <h2>If the chart is the part that is costing you time</h2>
        <p>Thirty days, no card. Or twenty minutes with a physician who uses it daily.</p>
        <a href={onboardUrl} class="btn btn-primary-lg">Start free trial</a>
        <a href="/book-demo/" class="btn btn-secondary-lg">Book a demo</a>
      </div>

    </div>
  </div>
</section>

</Page>
